\name{pride_list_ftp_files}
\alias{pride_list_ftp_files}
\title{The complete file list of a PRIDE Archive project, from its FTP directory tree}
\usage{
pride_list_ftp_files(accession, timeout = 300)
}
\arguments{
\item{accession}{A project accession, e.g. \code{"PXD000001"}.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow for the whole walk, which spans one request per directory.}

}
\description{
List every file a PRIDE project holds by walking its FTP directory tree, subdirectories included, with approximate sizes.

The authoritative counterpart to \code{\link{pride_list_files}}. Where that returns PRIDE's REST manifest -
knowingly incomplete for some projects, omitting for PXD000001 the two largest of 13 files - this
walks the project's FTP directory (subdirectories included) and returns everything the project
holds (mzLib #1121). Reach for it whenever completeness or a true project size matters.
}
\details{
This is a \strong{listing} surface only. \code{\link{pride_download}} and \code{\link{pride_download_files}} operate on the
REST manifest, so a file that appears *only* here - the whole point of this function - is fetched
directly from its \code{url} with an ordinary HTTPS client (e.g. \code{download.file(f$url, f$file_name)}).
}
\value{
A data.frame with one row per file and columns \code{relative_path}, \code{file_name}, \code{url},
  \code{approximate_size_bytes}, \code{approximate_size_mb}, and \code{extension}.

  Sizes are PRIDE's rounded directory-index values - good for a project-size estimate but not
  exact. For the precise transfer size of one file, issue an HTTP HEAD against its \code{url}. See
  \code{\link{pride_approximate_total_size_bytes}}.

  An unknown accession \strong{raises} \code{mzlib_project_not_found} (as \code{\link{pride_list_files}} does): mzLib
  resolves the project before walking, so a typo fails loudly rather than returning nothing.
}
